# 7. FIREBASE AUTHENTICATION

Replace PostgreSQL authentication completely.

Use:

```text
Firebase Authentication
```

for:

```text
signup
login
logout
password reset
email verification
authentication persistence
```

Remove production dependency on:

```text
bcrypt login queries
password_hash database fields
express-session
SESSION_SECRET
PostgreSQL session tables
```

The frontend must use Firebase Authentication appropriately.

For protected backend endpoints:

```text
Authorization: Bearer <Firebase ID token>
```

The backend must verify the token using:

```text
Firebase Admin SDK
verifyIdToken()
```

Never trust a `userId` supplied by the browser.

Use the verified:

```text
uid
```

as the identity.

---

# 8. EXISTING USER MIGRATION

If recoverable user data or database exports exist, provide a one-time migration mechanism.

If bcrypt password hashes are available and compatible with Firebase Authentication's supported bulk import mechanism, migrate users without requiring plaintext passwords.

Never put password hashes into Firestore.

If the original password hashes are unavailable, create the users safely and implement a password-reset/recovery workflow.

Do not invent user passwords.

Create:

```text
scripts/import-users-to-firebase.js
```

if source records exist.

Support:

```text
--dry-run
--execute
```

---

# 9. DATA RECOVERY POLICY

The final runtime must not depend on Render.

However, if the expired Render database can still be read temporarily, it may be used ONLY as a one-time migration source.

That does NOT mean Render remains part of production.

Allowed:

```text
Render PostgreSQL
     │
     │ one-time export
     ▼
migration script
     │
     ▼
Firebase
```

Not allowed:

```text
production frontend
     ↓
Render
```

If Render data cannot be accessed, migrate all available exports and document exactly which historical data could not be recovered.

Never fabricate missing family-tree data.

