# 14. FIRESTORE REPOSITORY LAYER

Replace SQL access with a clean repository layer.

Examples:

```javascript
getUser()
getTree()
getPersons()
getPerson()
createPerson()
updatePerson()
deletePerson()

getRelationships()
createRelationship()
deleteRelationship()
```

All ownership checks must use the authenticated Firebase UID.

Do not expose arbitrary Firestore admin access through APIs.

---

# 15. FAMILY TREE GRAPH INTEGRITY

The core domain is a graph.

Preserve relationship semantics.

For every relationship:

```text
person1 must exist
person2 must exist
both must belong to the correct tree
type must be valid
self relationships must be rejected where invalid
```

Deleting a person must safely handle associated relationships.

Firestore does not provide PostgreSQL-style foreign keys/cascade deletes.

Implement this intentionally using:

```text
batched writes
transactions
controlled server-side deletes
```

as appropriate.

---

# 16. TREE DELETION

If users can delete an entire family tree, remember that deleting a Firestore parent document does NOT automatically delete subcollections.

Implement safe recursive deletion through privileged server logic.

Do not leave orphaned:

```text
persons
relationships
members
photos
```

---

# 17. FIREBASE STORAGE

Move user photographs to:

```text
Cloud Storage for Firebase
```

Do not depend on:

```text
public/uploads
local server filesystem
Cloud Run ephemeral disk
```

Suggested layout:

```text
users/{uid}/trees/{treeId}/persons/{personId}/profile/<file>
```

or another clean structure.

Store the Storage object path in Firestore.

Do not make the bucket universally public.

---

# 18. STORAGE SECURITY RULES

Create:

```text
storage.rules
```

Users must only be able to access files belonging to trees they are authorized to access.

Validate:

```text
file size
content type
authorization
```

Prevent arbitrary executable uploads.

---

# 19. FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES

Create:

```text
firestore.rules
```

At minimum prove:

```text
anonymous user cannot read private trees
anonymous user cannot modify trees

User A can read User A data
User A can modify User A data

User A cannot read User B data
User A cannot modify User B data
```

If shared-family-tree functionality exists, implement membership-based authorization.

Do not use:

```text
allow read, write: if true;
```

in production.

---

# 20. FIRESTORE INDEXES

Create:

```text
firestore.indexes.json
```

Track all required composite indexes.

Do not depend on manually created undocumented indexes.

---

# 21. AUTHORIZATION

Separate:

```text
authentication
```

from:

```text
authorization
```

Firebase Authentication proves who the user is.

Firestore/Functions must determine what they may access.

Never accept:

```text
ownerUid
userId
role
isAdmin
```

from the frontend as proof of authorization.

---

# 22. ADMIN USERS

If admin functionality exists, implement it securely.

Use Firebase custom claims where appropriate.

Example:

```text
admin: true
```

must only be assigned server-side.

Never allow users to promote themselves through Firestore.

